1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,400
CLOUDFLARE and DOCKER both
show up in web projects.

2
00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:08,800
Treating them as ALTERNATIVES is like comparing a road network with a shipping BOX.

3
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:13,500
Docker packs an app with the PIECES
it needs: CODE, libraries, settings.

4
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:17,900
That package is an IMAGE. Run
it, and it becomes a CONTAINER.

5
00:00:17,900 --> 00:00:22,300
Cloudflare does a DIFFERENT job:
it sits in FRONT of your site.

6
00:00:22,300 --> 00:00:27,700
It can ROUTE visitors, keep copies of
pages near them, and BLOCK bad traffic.

7
00:00:27,700 --> 00:00:34,100
A team builds the app in Docker, runs it
on a SERVER, and puts Cloudflare in FRONT.

8
00:00:34,100 --> 00:00:38,300
Two LAYERS, two JOBS. The app is
packed, the traffic is handled.

9
00:00:38,300 --> 00:00:43,000
They are NOT COMPETITORS. Each
does what the other does NOT.

10
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:47,400
Cloudflare doesn't HOST your
app. Docker doesn't SECURE it.

11
00:00:47,400 --> 00:00:50,800
Docker PACKAGES the app. Cloudflare
delivers and PROTECTS it.

12
00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:54,000
Different jobs. Sometimes, ONE STACK.
